{"kind":"article","metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"ScreenStates"}],"roleHeading":"Article","title":"Localization","role":"article"},"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"level":2,"text":"Overview","anchor":"Overview","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The default Empty\/Loading\/Error placeholders’ text — “Nothing Here,” “Loading,” “Retry,” “Something Went Wrong,” “Connection Issue” — comes from a String Catalog, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Resources\/Localizable.xcstrings"},{"type":"text","text":", resolved from ScreenStates’ own bundle ("},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Bundle.module"},{"type":"text","text":") rather than your app’s. That’s deliberate: a custom title your app passes in (see below) is never run back through localization, and a language you add to "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"your own"}]},{"type":"text","text":" app’s catalog has no effect on these five strings — they only ever resolve against ScreenStates’ bundle. English, Spanish, French, and Russian are built in today."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"text":"Adding a language to ScreenStates","anchor":"Adding-a-language-to-ScreenStates","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"This is a change to the package itself, not something a consuming app can do on its own — it needs a PR. Open "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Resources\/Localizable.xcstrings"},{"type":"text","text":" and add a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"localizations"},{"type":"text","text":" entry for the new language code under each of the five string keys:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["\"Retry\" : {","  \"comment\" : \"Title of the button that retries loading after an error.\",","  \"localizations\" : {","    \"en\" : { \"stringUnit\" : { \"state\" : \"translated\", \"value\" : \"Retry\" } },","    \"de\" : { \"stringUnit\" : { \"state\" : \"translated\", \"value\" : \"Wiederholen\" } }","  }","}"],"syntax":"json","type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Every one of the five keys needs an entry for every language — a string present in English but missing a translation elsewhere fails CI (see below). "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStatesStrings.swift"},{"type":"text","text":"‘s doc comments list all five keys and their English text if you’re starting from scratch."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"text":"Overriding text per-app","anchor":"Overriding-text-per-app","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"You don’t need a new language, or a PR, to show different text than the built-in default — two options, depending on how much you want to replace:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView(title:)"},{"type":"text","text":" takes a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"title"},{"type":"text","text":" directly, bypassing "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Localizable.xcstrings"},{"type":"text","text":" entirely for that one string. Pass your own localized string (from your own app’s String Catalog, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"NSLocalizedString"},{"type":"text","text":", whatever) instead of the default:"}]},{"type":"codeListing","code":["ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView(title: String(localized: \"No Results\"))"],"syntax":"swift"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateDefaultErrorView"},{"type":"text","text":" has no equivalent for its title or Retry button text today — only the error’s own message is customizable, via "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDisplayableError","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"For anything else — the Error placeholder’s title, the Retry button’s text, or any text at all in a shape the built-in views don’t expose a parameter for — pass a fully custom placeholder instead of the default one, via "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateView","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":"’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"empty"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"loading"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"error"},{"type":"text","text":" builder closures (see "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithUIKit","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":") or "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateViewController","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":"‘s equivalent. A custom placeholder can still reuse "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState\/accessibilityAnnouncement","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" so VoiceOver announces transitions with the same wording your custom text uses, instead of the default’s."}]}]}],"type":"unorderedList"}]}],"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"patch":0,"major":0},"sections":[],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Localization"},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates"]]},"seeAlsoSections":[{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithUIKit","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Accessibility"],"anchor":"Related-Documentation","title":"Related Documentation"}],"abstract":[{"text":"How the default placeholders’ built-in text is localized, how to add a language, and how to show different text instead without touching ScreenStates itself.","type":"text"}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/screenstates\/localization"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"references":{"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/ScreenStateDisplayableError":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDisplayableError","title":"ScreenStateDisplayableError","role":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"ScreenStateDisplayableError"}],"type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Lets an error type supply its own user-facing message, distinct from"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"localizedDescription"},{"type":"text","text":" — which is often a debugging string (or, for a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"plain "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Error"},{"type":"text","text":" with no "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"CustomStringConvertible"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"LocalizedError"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"conformance, just the type name) rather than something to put in front"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"of the person using the app."}],"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/screenstatedisplayableerror","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"protocol"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ScreenStateDisplayableError"}]},"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/Accessibility":{"kind":"article","url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/accessibility","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Accessibility","role":"article","title":"Accessibility","type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Everything the default placeholders — and your own custom ones — get for free: VoiceOver, Reduce Motion, Increase Contrast, Dynamic Type, and stable identifiers for XCUITest."}]},"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/GettingStartedWithUIKit":{"kind":"article","url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/gettingstartedwithuikit","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithUIKit","role":"article","title":"Getting Started with UIKit","type":"topic","abstract":[{"text":"Drive a UIKit screen’s four states with ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","isActive":true},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateContainerView","isActive":true},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/ScreenStateViewController":{"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/screenstateviewcontroller","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"ScreenStateViewController"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateViewController","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"class"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ScreenStateViewController"}],"role":"symbol","title":"ScreenStateViewController","type":"topic","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"A "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIViewController"},{"type":"text","text":" base class that owns a "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateContainerView"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"pinned to the view’s edges and bound to a "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore"},{"type":"text","text":","},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"wrapping the boilerplate every UIKit screen using Scr